Three days ago, Jarred Sumner published a blog post about using an AI Agent to rewrite Bun from Zig to Rust.
The entire rewrite took only a few days. A JavaScript runtime, ported from one language to another — with an AI Agent doing the vast majority of the work.
What&rsquo;s shocking isn&rsquo;t &ldquo;AI can write Rust now.&rdquo; It&rsquo;s that this fundamentally changes our definition of &ldquo;understanding code.&rdquo;
In the past, a developer needed months to understand Bun&rsquo;s codebase — reading source, following issues, submitting patches. Now, an AI can traverse the entire codebase in minutes, generate replacement plans, and even pass the full test suite.
The question is: when AI writes code far faster than we can read it, does &ldquo;understanding&rdquo; still matter?
0. The Meta-Question: When Writing Code Is No Longer Scarce, What&rsquo;s Your Moat? In early July 2026, the AI Engineer World&rsquo;s Fair convened in San Francisco. Among over 300 talks, one concept from Geoffrey Litt resonated widely.
He wasn&rsquo;t talking about prompt engineering, or tool calling. He was talking about something that sounds almost cliché — &ldquo;understanding.&rdquo;
But he phrased it differently. The term he used was Understand to participate.
The challenge Geoffrey faces: when Coding Agents start independently completing increasingly large-scale code changes, your understanding inevitably drifts away from the code&rsquo;s actual state. This accumulated gap is cognitive debt.
Cognitive debt is not the same as technical debt. Technical debt lives in the code — tight coupling, missing tests, confusing naming. Cognitive debt lives in your mind — a gap between your mental model of the code and the code&rsquo;s actual state.
What&rsquo;s worse: cognitive debt has no compiler error. You won&rsquo;t get a warning saying &ldquo;your understanding of this module is 3 weeks out of date.&rdquo; You&rsquo;ll only discover it three weeks later when you attempt a seemingly simple change and realize the entire system no longer looks the way you imagined.
Simon Willison added a key observation when sharing Geoffrey&rsquo;s talk:
&ldquo;You need a sufficiently deep understanding to be an actively creative participant. If you lack a rich enough set of concepts in your mind, your ability to participate in the project is concretely limited.&rdquo;
Translated into engineer-speak: AI can write code for you, but it can&rsquo;t think about architecture for you.
1. &ldquo;Reading Code&rdquo; Went from Basic Skill to Core Competency Ten years ago, one difference between a senior and junior engineer was &ldquo;code reading speed.&rdquo;
A senior engineer could glance at a piece of code and immediately know what it does, where the problems are, and what a change would affect. A junior engineer had to grind through it line by line.
Now, a junior engineer&rsquo;s AI assistant can &ldquo;read code&rdquo; too. Give Claude Code the full repository context and it can instantly tell you the call chain of any function, or the likely root cause of any bug.
So &ldquo;reading code&rdquo; has been split in two.
One half is what AI can do: fast scanning, pattern matching, dependency analysis. This half has been thoroughly commoditized.
The other half is what AI can&rsquo;t yet do: judging what matters and what doesn&rsquo;t.
Geoffrey&rsquo;s original words:
&ldquo;You need a rich set of concepts in your mind to think creatively and fluently about how to move something forward.&rdquo;
In other words: you need a concept network in your head to think creatively about the next step.
This isn&rsquo;t about &ldquo;memorizing API docs&rdquo; — AI does that a hundred times better than you. It&rsquo;s about knowing when to question a design decision, when to accept a tradeoff, and when to tear something down and start over.
Lesson 1: AI eliminates the barrier of &ldquo;can I understand this,&rdquo; shifting the competitive focus to &ldquo;do I know what&rsquo;s worth understanding.&rdquo;
2. Bun&rsquo;s Zig→Rust Rewrite: A Textbook Case Back to the Bun story. Jarred Sumner wrote this in his blog:
&ldquo;Until recently, the choice of programming language for a project like Bun was a one-way ticket. Everyone knows you shouldn&rsquo;t stop and rewrite a large piece of software from scratch. Joel Spolsky said back in 2000 that it&rsquo;s the single worst thing you can do.&rdquo;
But Coding Agents changed the equation.
The dilemma the Bun team faced was concrete: Zig&rsquo;s handling of mixed GC and manual memory management produced endless use-after-free, double-free, and &ldquo;forgot to free in the error path&rdquo; bugs.
Jarred said: &ldquo;I got tired of going to bed every night worrying Bun would crash.&rdquo;
In safe Rust, these bugs become compiler errors. RAII-style automatic Drop cleanup inherently solves the resource leak problem.
But what really made the rewrite possible was a non-technical factor: Bun&rsquo;s test suite was written in TypeScript.
This meant the test suite was implementation-language-agnostic. Swap the implementation from Zig to Rust, and the tests still run. This consistent suite became the automated acceptance criteria for the AI Agent — &ldquo;translate this Zig code to Rust, run the tests, try again if they fail.&rdquo;
Lesson 2: The role of the test suite changes in the Agent era — it&rsquo;s no longer just a quality guard, but the Agent&rsquo;s &ldquo;autonomous learning feedback signal.&rdquo;
Simon Willison, when commenting on this in his blog, pointed out another key aspect:
Jarred started the rewrite as an experiment — he just wanted to try the then-very-early Fable model. &ldquo;At first I didn&rsquo;t think it would work. A few days later, a high percentage of functionality was already running.&rdquo;
This reveals a subtle psychological shift: the core of Agent engineering is no longer &ldquo;can I do this,&rdquo; but &ldquo;am I willing to try.&rdquo;
3. Three Faces of Cognitive Debt Putting Geoffrey Litt&rsquo;s and Jarred Sumner&rsquo;s stories side by side, cognitive debt manifests in AI programming in three ways:
Type Symptoms Solution Understanding Lag AI produces piles of code; you can&rsquo;t keep up reading it Geoffrey&rsquo;s &ldquo;understand to participate&rdquo; — don&rsquo;t aim to understand every line, aim to understand architectural decisions Decision Outsourcing AI picks solutions for you; you don&rsquo;t know why Make AI explain the tradeoff, rather than giving you the answer directly Confidence Bubble All tests pass, so you feel safe Use AI for adversarial review — scrutinize your own code from an attacker&rsquo;s perspective The third is a technique used in the Bun rewrite: Jarred had one Agent write code and another Agent do adversarial review. Let AIs challenge each other, not just cooperate with you.
This actually circles back to Geoffrey&rsquo;s core argument: the purpose of understanding is not &ldquo;comprehending,&rdquo; but &ldquo;being able to participate.&rdquo;
You don&rsquo;t need to understand every line of code. But at key decision points — architecture choices, interface design, error handling strategies — you must be able to say &ldquo;this part is wrong, let&rsquo;s try a different approach.&rdquo;
Lesson 3: &ldquo;Comprehending&rdquo; is passive; &ldquo;participating&rdquo; is active. The decisive factor in AI programming isn&rsquo;t how fast you learn, but whether you dare to say &ldquo;no&rdquo; at key decision points.
4. Three Things You Should Do First, shift Code Review&rsquo;s focus from &ldquo;finding bugs&rdquo; to &ldquo;finding decisions.&rdquo;
In the past, when reviewing code, we watched for potential null pointers, boundary conditions, and performance hotspots. After AI starts writing code, these low-level errors actually become rarer. The real danger is — AI makes an architectural assumption somewhere that seems reasonable but is fundamentally wrong, and that assumption influences all subsequent implementation.
The Claude Code team shared a suggestion at AIE: let Fable use its own judgment to decide when to write tests, rather than telling it &ldquo;only write tests for big features.&rdquo; The same logic applies to review — you shouldn&rsquo;t review every line, but every architectural decision.
Second, design your test suite as the Agent&rsquo;s learning environment.
Bun&rsquo;s TypeScript test suite is a perfect example. It&rsquo;s independent of the implementation language, giving the Agent an objective right/wrong standard. If you&rsquo;re working on a project, make sure your integration tests aren&rsquo;t tightly coupled to the current implementation — they should be &ldquo;behavioral specifications,&rdquo; not &ldquo;implementation snapshots.&rdquo;
Third, do regular &ldquo;reverse understanding&rdquo; checks.
The core problem with cognitive debt, as Geoffrey describes it, is that you don&rsquo;t know what you don&rsquo;t know. One practical approach: every two weeks, pick a module you let AI write, and without looking at the code, sketch its architecture and data flow on a whiteboard. If you can&rsquo;t draw it, or if what you draw doesn&rsquo;t match reality — that&rsquo;s the interest on your cognitive debt compounding.
Summary Decision The Fundamental Question It Answers Shift review focus from bugs to decisions The purpose of understanding isn&rsquo;t finding errors — it&rsquo;s participating Test suite as Agent learning environment Is the feedback signal you give AI objective enough? Reverse understanding checks How do you know what you don&rsquo;t know? Adversarial review Is your confidence a bubble? Lesson 3: &ldquo;Comprehending&rdquo; is passive; &ldquo;participating&rdquo; is active. The decisive factor in AI programming isn&rsquo;t how fast you learn, but whether you dare to say &ldquo;no&rdquo; at key decision points.
The biggest change AI programming brings isn&rsquo;t a new tool. It transforms the scarcest resource in software engineering from &ldquo;time to write code&rdquo; into &ldquo;mental bandwidth to understand code.&rdquo;
In the past, your productivity depended on how much code you could write. Now, your productivity depends on how deeply you can think alongside AI — and the prerequisite for thinking is understanding.
