Serde Macros Made Your Rust Service 10x Slower? Old Zhang&rsquo;s Story Yesterday afternoon, Old Zhang rushed to my desk with his laptop, looking like he&rsquo;d just swallowed a lemon.
&ldquo;Bro, help me out here. My Rust service is acting weird. The code looks fine, but once it&rsquo;s deployed, it&rsquo;s slow as molasses.&rdquo;
I took his laptop and skimmed through the code. Honestly, at first glance, nothing seemed wrong. Rust is fast by nature - everyone knows that. It&rsquo;s like Usain Bolt - even if you tried to slow him down, it&rsquo;d be tough.
&ldquo;Tell me the symptoms first,&rdquo; I said to Old Zhang.
&ldquo;It&rsquo;s that debug logging feature,&rdquo; Old Zhang pointed at a piece of code. &ldquo;Product wanted to log every request payload for troubleshooting later. I thought it was simple - just add a serde_json::to_string and call it a day.&rdquo;
I leaned in closer. Holy cow, this guy was doing JSON serialization in every single request processing flow.
&ldquo;How many requests per second does this service handle?&rdquo; I asked.
&ldquo;We stress-tested it - around 50,000,&rdquo; Old Zhang said.
I paused for a moment, then said: &ldquo;Old Zhang, have you thought about what it means when you serialize the entire data structure 50,000 times per second?&rdquo;
Old Zhang froze.
I gave him an analogy: &ldquo;Imagine you run a package delivery station. Normally, you just glance at the address on the package and toss it into the right basket. That&rsquo;s fast, right?&rdquo;
Old Zhang nodded.
&ldquo;Now,&rdquo; I continued, &ldquo;for &lsquo;future reference,&rsquo; every time you receive a package, you open it up, take out everything inside, photograph each item, then repack it. You think that won&rsquo;t slow you down?&rdquo;
Old Zhang&rsquo;s expression shifted from confusion to enlightenment.
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s the issue with Serde macros,&rdquo; I explained. &ldquo;Look at this line: #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]. Looks harmless at first glance. But this macro generates a ton of code at compile time, and that code does a lot of work at runtime.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Like what?&rdquo; Old Zhang started paying attention.
&ldquo;For example, if your struct has a String, serialization makes a copy. If you have a Vec with 10,000 numbers, serialization converts each number to a string and allocates memory for the JSON array. These operations seem trivial, but at 50,000 requests per second, the memory allocation frequency goes through the roof.&rdquo;
I showed Old Zhang a piece of code:
use serde::{Serialize, Deserialize}; use serde_json; #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Debug)] struct Payload { id: u64, name: String, values: Vec&lt;u64&gt;, } fn main() { let payload = Payload { id: 42, name: &#34;SuperLongStringThatKeepsAllocating&#34;.to_string(), values: (0..10_000).collect(), }; // Innocent-looking serialization for _ in 0..1000 { let _ = serde_json::to_string(&amp;payload).unwrap(); } } &ldquo;See, every loop iteration: allocates memory for the entire Vec as a JSON array, copies the name string, formats numbers into strings. Multiply that by your request rate, and you&rsquo;ve just built a bottleneck factory.&rdquo;
Old Zhang rubbed his chin: &ldquo;So what do I do? I can&rsquo;t just remove logging.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Of course not,&rdquo; I said. &ldquo;Think about it - do you really need to serialize on every request?&rdquo;
Old Zhang thought for a moment: &ldquo;Not really. I only need to log when there&rsquo;s an error.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Exactly! So why not move the serialization into the error branch? Normal cases don&rsquo;t execute it at all - only serialize when there&rsquo;s an error. Much faster.&rdquo;
Old Zhang&rsquo;s eyes lit up: &ldquo;Makes sense.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Also,&rdquo; I added, &ldquo;look at your struct - it has several Strings. You could change them to &amp;str. That way, serialization doesn&rsquo;t copy - it just borrows the original reference.&rdquo;
I wrote him an optimized version:
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)] struct Payload&lt;&#39;a&gt; { id: u64, name: &amp;&#39;a str, // Use reference instead of String } &ldquo;That&rsquo;s like&hellip;&rdquo; Old Zhang pondered.
&ldquo;Like photocopying documents,&rdquo; I jumped in. &ldquo;String is like making a fresh copy every time. &amp;str is like just showing someone the original - no copying needed. Which is faster?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Got it, got it,&rdquo; Old Zhang nodded repeatedly. &ldquo;Any other tricks?&rdquo;
&ldquo;Sure,&rdquo; I said. &ldquo;Think about it - your logs are for your own eyes, not for other systems to parse. Why use JSON? Use something like Bincode - a binary format that&rsquo;s way faster.&rdquo;
&ldquo;What&rsquo;s Bincode?&rdquo;
&ldquo;It&rsquo;s a binary serialization format, much faster than JSON - about 5-6 times faster.&rdquo; I showed him:
// Use Bincode instead of JSON let encoded = bincode::serialize(&amp;payload).unwrap(); &ldquo;It&rsquo;s like taking notes: writing Chinese characters by hand versus typing with a keyboard. Obviously typing is faster.&rdquo;
Old Zhang immediately went back to fix his code.
About half an hour later, Old Zhang came back, grinning from ear to ear.
&ldquo;Bro, it&rsquo;s magic! I moved serialization to the error branch, changed String to &amp;str, and switched to Bincode. Ran a stress test - performance is back, and CPU usage dropped by half. The optimization effect is so obvious.&rdquo;
I patted his shoulder: &ldquo;Remember this - Rust itself is fast, but even the fastest car struggles if you stuff two tons of bricks in the trunk. Serde macros aren&rsquo;t bad - they&rsquo;re great tools. But you need to understand what they&rsquo;re doing.&rdquo;
Old Zhang nodded repeatedly: &ldquo;Learned my lesson.&rdquo;
Here&rsquo;s a summary - if you run into similar issues, here&rsquo;s how to troubleshoot:
Step one: Make sure you&rsquo;re compiling in release mode. Debug mode being slow is normal.
Step two: Use cargo flamegraph to see where CPU time is going. If you see serde_json or various clone functions taking up a lot, that&rsquo;s probably your issue.
Step three: Check your code - are you serializing in places where you don&rsquo;t need to? If data is just passing around inside your program, just pass references. Don&rsquo;t go the JSON route.
Step four: If you really need serialization, consider faster formats like Bincode or MessagePack.
Step five: Don&rsquo;t slap Serialize and Deserialize on every big struct. Create a lightweight DTO specifically for serialization, containing only the fields you need to serialize.
That&rsquo;s how coding works - seemingly simple operations can hide massive overhead. Serde macros are convenient, like an all-you-can-eat buffet. But remember - eat too much and you&rsquo;ll gain weight.
